23.1
π-finite Spaces

Now, for a pointed topological space Z, we define PZ to be the space of continuous paths
[0, 1] →Z, and then we have the path space fibration π : PZ →Z, given by evaluation of
the path at t = 1. Note that PZ is contractible and that the fibers are homotopy equivalent
to ΩZ. Apply this to Z = K(A, n + 1). Then the fibers of π : PK(A, n + 1) →K(A, n + 1)
are Eilenberg-Maclane spaces K(A, n) and hence, the path fibration is the analog of the
universal bundle for the H-space K(A, n). Therefore, the classifying space is:

BK(A, n) ∼= K(A, n + 1) .
(23.13)

It follows from (23.13) that fibrations over X with fiber K(A, n) are classified by homotopy
classes of maps,

X −→K(A, n + 1) .
(23.14)

Since K(A, 1) = BA is the classifying space of principal A-bundles, equation (23.13) shows
that we can write K(A, n) = BnA. This notation emphasizes the role of the Eilenberg-
MacLane spaces as classifying spaces and we will sometimes use it.

Definition 23.1 (π-finite space). A π-finite space X is a topological space with a finite set
of connected components, each of which has a finite set of nonzero homotopy groups πj(X),
each of which is a finite group.

When X is connected, it is homotopy equivalent to a “Postnikov tower,” which is an
iterated fibration of Eilenberg-MacLane spaces (see Bott-Tu, p. 250-251 [BT82]):

...

K(π3, q3)
X (3)

K(π2, q2)
X (2)
BK(π3, q3) ∼= K(π3, q3 + 1)

X (1) = K(π1, q1)
BK(π2, q2) ∼= K(π2, q2 + 1)

...

(23.15)

The horizontal arrows are classifying maps of the K(πk, qk) bundle over X (k−1). So from
(23.14), we see that the fibration X (k) →X (k−1) is classified by the homotopy classes of maps
X (k−1) →K(πk+1, qk+1+1). A (defining) property of Eilenberg-MacLane spaces implies that

